"The prognosis is influenced by several adverse factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.3. The surgical findings indicate a high-risk profile, specifically a pathological stage of pT3b, positive surgical margins, invasion of the seminal vesicles, lymphovascular invasion, and documented lymph node metastasis. The biopsy findings showed mixed Gleason patterns (4+3 and 3+5) with cribriform and intraductal carcinoma.\n\nCountervailing factors are less defined by the provided data, but the documented nodal status is positive. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, which adds complexity. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding future trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.09 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 33.67–50.51 months; its coverage has not been validated."
